fact template_research {
  title = "Template — research"
  audience = :both
  related = []
  body {
    p <<DOC
      A **research** unit is a dated finding from an investigation: what was asked, what was found,
      when it was last verified, and what backs it. It is the one unit kind that carries an expiry —
      `checked` plus `status` say whether it can still be trusted. Its `audience` defaults to `:ai`,
      so findings reach the skill and stay out of the book unless you opt in with `:both`.
    DOC

    h2 "Skeleton"
    code "wcl" {
      source = <<DOC
        // data/research/<id>.wcl
        research <id> {
          topic      = "<Headline of the finding>"
          question   = "<What the investigation set out to answer.>"
          summary    = "<The finding, in one sentence.>"
          status     = :current            // :current | :stale | :superseded
          checked    = "<YYYY-MM-DD>"      // the date the finding was last verified
          applies_to = "<subject/version the finding holds for>"
          source_ids = [<source block ids>]
          locators   = ["<url or path not worth a source block>"]
          audience   = :ai                 // :both to also put it in the book
          related    = [<up to ~5 ids>]

          body {
            p "<The finding, stated first.>"
            p "<How it was established — what was run, read, or measured.>"

            table {
              header = ["Claim", "Evidence"]
              rows = [
                ["<claim>", "<what backs it>"],
              ]
            }
          }
        }
      DOC
    }

    h2 "Fields"
    table {
      header = ["Field", "What goes in it"]
      rows = [["`question`", "The question as asked, before you knew the answer. It is what makes a stale finding re-checkable"], ["`summary`", "The finding itself, not \"investigated X\""], ["`checked`", "An ISO date. This is the field that decides whether the next reader trusts it"], ["`applies_to`", "The version or subject it holds for — a finding about `bevy 0.18` is wrong about `0.19` and should say so"], ["`status`", "`:stale` when the date is old and nobody re-checked; `:superseded` with `superseded_by` when a newer unit replaces it"], ["`source_ids` / `locators`", "Where it came from. A finding with neither is an assertion"]]
    }
    p <<DOC
      Research is a staging area, not the destination: once a finding is settled, fold it into a
      concept, fact or procedure and leave the research unit as the provenance record.
    DOC

    h2 "Filled example"
    code "wcl" {
      source = <<DOC
        research skill_frontmatter_limits {
          topic      = "SKILL.md front-matter length limits"
          question   = "How long can a skill's name and description be before Claude Code truncates them?"
          summary    = "The description is truncated in the picker past roughly 1024 characters; name past 64."
          status     = :current
          checked    = "2026-07-27"
          applies_to = "Claude Code 2.x"
          locators   = ["docs/wskills/wskill/out/skill/SKILL.md"]
          audience   = :both

          body {
            p "Descriptions longer than about 1024 characters are cut in the skill picker, and names longer than 64 are elided mid-word. Both are display limits: the full text still loads."
            p "Measured by rendering a skill with progressively longer front matter and reading it back from the picker."
          }
        }
      DOC
    }

    h2 "Done when"
    list {
      li "`checked` is the date you actually verified it, not the date you wrote it."
      li "`summary` states the finding, not the activity."
      li "Something backs it: a source id, a locator, or a described measurement."
      li "`applies_to` pins the version or subject when the finding is version-sensitive."
    }
  }
}
